    "source_file_to_entries": {
        "raw-wiktextract-data.jsonl": [
            {
                "entry_id": "b79adb1b-de62-30d1-81c3-068a311ddbe4",
                "word": "coursed",
                "pos": "adj",
                "lang_code": "en",
                "forms": [],
                "senses": [
                    {
                        "sense_id": "1098b57e-ea39-3efa-b667-f00e6de1f365",
                        "entry_id": "b79adb1b-de62-30d1-81c3-068a311ddbe4",
                        "glosses": [
                            "Laid out in courses or rows."
                        ],
                        "tags": [],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "36f47266-27e9-41e5-ace7-9a2109337480",
                                "sense_id": "1098b57e-ea39-3efa-b667-f00e6de1f365",
                                "text": "For example, when a coursed rubble or block-in-course wing-wall or spandril of a bridge has to support an ashlar parapet, a string course must first be placed on the wall, to give a steady base for the parapet, and to distribute its weight over the smaller stones below."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "f2bd6955-8d90-4a6d-900d-6c9b0f7719bc",
                                "sense_id": "1098b57e-ea39-3efa-b667-f00e6de1f365",
                                "text": "A coursed-ashlar wall is shown in Fig. 1, and a broken-ashlar wall in Fig . 2."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "754cbc7f-c81c-4030-92a3-28bfc1e5f86c",
                                "sense_id": "1098b57e-ea39-3efa-b667-f00e6de1f365",
                                "text": "The Thomas Bowman Barn No. 2 is a large two-and-a-half-story structure of wood frame construction built on a coursed stone foundation."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "3bd9ce1d-e3c6-445e-a742-a0a15be16e65",
                                "sense_id": "1098b57e-ea39-3efa-b667-f00e6de1f365",
                                "text": "It [St Pinnock Viaduct] has seven piers of roughly-dressed, coursed slatestone from Westwood quarry."
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "sense_id": "3b17210a-8a0e-3049-8ac2-058ce8eb0489",
                        "entry_id": "b79adb1b-de62-30d1-81c3-068a311ddbe4",
                        "glosses": [
                            "Chased by hunters with dogs."
                        ],
                        "tags": [],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "21013555-947b-44ce-829a-423d53b9ed17",
                                "sense_id": "3b17210a-8a0e-3049-8ac2-058ce8eb0489",
                                "text": "Daniel, in his Rural Sports (vol. i. p. 446, and Suppl. p. 694(, mentions several instances of white hares; and Scott, in his British Field Sports (p. 361), gives an account of a coursed hare which was said to have become white from fright!"
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "5361c112-37be-45ab-a982-4428823775b9",
                                "sense_id": "3b17210a-8a0e-3049-8ac2-058ce8eb0489",
                                "text": "By this means it will be found little, if at all, inferior to a coursed or hunted hare."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "6c5af81e-35f0-43b3-97f9-ac4b4b3f3fb2",
                                "sense_id": "3b17210a-8a0e-3049-8ac2-058ce8eb0489",
                                "text": "One point, however, is clear:—if the brush of a coursed fox is to be awareded according to the rules with a hunted fox, \"Brush\" is the very last man in the field that can claim it; for it appears that when Reynard was killed, \"Brush\" was coursing a fresh fox in an opposite direction!"
                            }
                        ]
                    }
                ],
                "translations": [],
                "word_linkages": []
            },
